WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.100
Welcome to fascinating frontiers, episode 77.

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:05.780
It's May 22nd, 2026.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.880
The universe has been busy, let's get into it.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:11.320
SpaceX halted the first flight

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:14.540
of its heavily redesigned Starship VIII vehicle

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:17.300
after a last minute ground equipment problem.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.940
NASA plans to share details on its evolving approach

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:24.620
to a sustained lunar presence and the missions that will follow.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:28.060
The update will outline how the agency intends to move

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:31.600
from initial landings toward longer-term surface operations.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:34.620
Officials are expected to discuss timelines

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:37.580
for habitat development and resource utilization.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:41.960
This briefing comes as NASA continues to refine its architecture

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:43.900
for returning astronauts to the moon

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.340
and eventually sending crews to Mars.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.240
Watch for the specific milestones and technology priorities

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:51.740
that emerge from the announcement.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:54.740
While NASA maps its next decade on the moon,

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.160
another nation is already celebrating

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:58.700
a major lunar milestone.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:02.600
India's Chandraayan 3 mission has been recognized

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.820
with a top international space award

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:07.200
for its successful lunar South Pole landing.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.720
The achievement marked the first time

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.540
a spacecraft touched down near the moon's South Pole.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.440
The mission demonstrated precise navigation

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:18.500
and landing capabilities under challenging conditions.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:21.120
Organizers highlighted the engineering

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.460
and scientific contributions that advanced lunar exploration

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:25.300
for all nations.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:27.980
Follow-up coverage will likely detail

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:30.980
how the award reflects growing international cooperation

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:32.200
in lunar science.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.700
As lunar successes multiply,

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.540
it's worth looking back at the missions

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:39.420
that first showed us how to explore another planet.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.980
A science symposium at NASA Ames on July 20th

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:45.620
will celebrate the 50th anniversary

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:47.360
of the Viking missions to Mars.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:49.840
The event will spotlight contributions

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.760
from Ames scientists across multiple disciplines.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:56.500
Participants will hear directly from Viking team members

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.420
about the original mission's findings.

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:00.720
Discussions will also cover

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.720
how Vikings legacy shapes current

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:04.860
and future Mars exploration efforts.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:08.500
The symposium will be live streamed for public viewing.

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:11.260
While we honor past Mars achievements,

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.420
a very different kind of vehicle

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:16.480
is trying to rewrite how we reach space in the first place.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.780
SpaceX called off the first launch attempt

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:22.220
of its new block three starship vehicle

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.400
due to a ground equipment problem.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:27.260
The scrub occurred on May 21st

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:28.980
during final countdown preparations.

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:31.220
Teams are now assessing the issue

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:33.040
before scheduling the next attempt.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.940
The vehicle represents a major redesign

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.000
compared with the previous 11 flights.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.960
Observer's note that early test flights

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.680
of new versions often encounter such setbacks.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:46.780
Now, here's something that blew my mind

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:47.720
when I looked into it.

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:51.220
If the sun were a basketball in downtown Toronto,

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:54.820
the nearest star would sit another basketball in Vancouver.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.420
Yet light from that star still takes more than four years

00:02:58.420 --> 00:02:59.320
to reach us.

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:01.800
Imagine riding alongside a photon

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:05.320
that left a distant galaxy when dinosaurs still walked Earth.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:07.740
By the time it arrives at our telescopes,

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.040
the universe has aged billions of years

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:12.120
while the photon has experienced none.

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:16.580
Light from the Milky Way Center 26,000 light years away

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:18.560
shows us the galaxy as it looked

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:21.240
when early humans were first using stone tools.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:25.040
We can see entire galaxies whose light began its journey

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:27.320
before our own solar system formed.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:30.720
Yet we cannot know what those galaxies look like right now.

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:34.160
The unanswered question that keeps astronomers awake

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:37.580
is whether any signal or structure in that ancient light

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:40.160
can ever reveal what those same galaxies

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:41.980
are doing at this exact moment.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:45.640
Satellite views reveal the jagged scar left

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:48.480
by a powerful tornado that struck Southern Mississippi

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:49.440
in early May.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:53.020
The track shows clear patterns of damaged vegetation

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:54.180
across the landscape.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:57.080
Multiple twisters touched down in the region

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:59.140
during the same severe weather outbreak.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:01.920
The imagery helps emergency responders

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:04.320
and researchers map the storm's footprint.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:06.800
Similar Earth-observing data

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.480
continues to support rapid disaster assessment.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.660
Earth monitoring also delivers striking views

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:14.840
of our planet's most extreme landscapes.

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:18.340
New images from the Copernicus Sentinel-2 mission

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:20.260
show the sandy and rocky terrain

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:22.460
of Central Algeria's Sahara Desert.

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:26.060
The satellite data highlights contrasting textures

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:28.020
of dunes and rocky plateaus.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:31.460
Such observations contribute to ongoing studies

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:34.160
of arid land forms and surface processes.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:37.500
The mission routinely provides high resolution

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.280
views of Earth-changing landscapes.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:43.160
Researchers use these scenes to track desert expansion

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:44.580
and resource management.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.220
Shifting from our own planet to the skies overhead,

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:50.540
observers can watch IO

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:53.640
and another Galilean moon transit Jupiter this evening.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.740
East Coast viewers have the best early evening

00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:58.300
window shortly after sunset.

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:01.220
The events offer a chance to see the moons

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:04.600
as small dark silhouettes against the planet's bright face.

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:06.880
Jupiter remains well-placed

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:08.940
for telescopic viewing throughout the night.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:12.040
Mercury begins emerging from the sun's glare

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:14.760
and becomes visible low in the pre-dawn sky.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:17.580
Venus and Jupiter appear together

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:19.900
in the western sky shortly after sunset

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:21.060
during early July.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:25.040
In the morning sky, Saturn rises earlier each week

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:26.960
and pairs with Neptune in Pisces.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.620
The large constellation Ophiuchus

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:31.660
contains numerous globular clusters

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:33.840
and planetary nebulae worth hunting

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:34.620
with a telescope.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:38.560
The constellation Aura sits directly beneath Scorpius

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:41.040
and contains several attractive star clusters.

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:44.860
Binoculars reveal the clusters as small knots of light

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:47.440
against the rich star fields of the Milky Way.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:51.100
An observer at an Ohio astronomy club site

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:53.040
watched a stationary bright light

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:55.280
that later revealed itself as an airplane.

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:59.140
The object drifted slowly before the sound of jet engines

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:00.440
confirmed its identity.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.640
Such sightings are common near urban areas

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:04.980
with busy flight paths.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:07.560
Learning to distinguish aircraft flights

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:09.920
from celestial objects is a useful skill

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:11.140
for new stargazers.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:13.740
The experience illustrates how context

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:15.920
and patience help solve sky mysteries.

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:18.260
Next time, we'll be watching

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:20.900
for further updates on the Starship test program

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:23.480
and how the Block 3 redesign performs

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:25.160
on its next flight attempt.

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:28.060
That's fascinating frontiers for today.

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:30.400
If you enjoyed this, a rating or review

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:32.360
on Apple podcasts or Spotify

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:34.680
really helps new listeners find the show.

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:36.660
I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:39.360
Thanks for exploring with me and I'll see you next time.

